2. Free permissions

2.1 Subject to the conditions of this License, Tilion Inc. grants you a
worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free copyright license to copy, inspect,
modify, create derivative works of, and redistribute the Licensed Software
in source or binary form, and to use it for any non-production purpose.

2.2 Non-production use is free regardless of funding, revenue, size, or
whether the user is a commercial business. An Organization whose Cumulative
Funding is US$500,000 or more, or whose Annual Recurring Revenue exceeds
US$300,000, or both, may use the Licensed Software for non-production
development, testing, evaluation, proofs of concept, demonstrations,
education, and research without a subscription and without contacting
Tilion Inc. These permissions extend to employees, contractors, and service
providers performing those non-production activities on that Organization's
behalf.

2.3 The Licensed Software requires a license key to run. A free key is issued
to any person who signs in at https://tilion.dev/activate with an email
address, GitHub account, or Google account. No payment or sales contact is
required for permitted use. A key is verified on the user's machine and is
not checked online. The Licensed Software contains no telemetry, usage
reporting, heartbeat, or update poll, and makes no connection to any host
Tilion Inc. controls other than (a) obtaining or refreshing a key when the
user runs that command, and (b) downloading a release from the source the
user configures. Activation transmits to Tilion Inc. the sign-in email
address and provider and no information about use of the Licensed Software.
A machine without a key may run the Licensed Software for 14 days from its
first launch.

2.4 Production use is authorized only as provided in Section 3 or in a
separate written agreement with Tilion Inc.

3. Production use

3.1 The Paid-License Trigger applies only to production use. It does not
limit the non-production permissions in Section 2, whether or not an
Organization meets either or both financial thresholds.

3.2 You may make production use of the Licensed Software if any of the
following applies:

  (a) you are an individual using it solely for personal, non-commercial
      purposes and not on behalf of a business or other organization;

  (b) your Organization does not meet the Paid-License Trigger;

  (c) the use falls within a Grace Period under Section 4;

  (d) the use is covered by a current Fortress subscription under the
      Fortress Subscription Terms; or

  (e) the use is settled under Section 6 or falls within the Lapse Grace
      Period, in which case it is permitted from the day it began and no
      breach arises from it.

3.3 "Paid-License Trigger" means that your Organization's Cumulative Funding
is US$500,000 or more, or its Annual Recurring Revenue exceeds US$300,000.
Exactly US$500,000 in Cumulative Funding meets the Paid-License Trigger.
Exactly US$300,000 in Annual Recurring Revenue does not exceed its
threshold.

3.4 Subscription. An Organization that meets the Paid-License Trigger
requires one Fortress subscription. A subscription covers production use by
the whole Organization, with no limit on people, machines, containers,
agents, or browsers. The list price is the same for every subscribing
Organization and is published at https://tilion.com/pricing; a separate
written agreement may provide a lower price. A subscription runs in terms of
three months and renews automatically. Tilion Inc. may change the price or
the Fortress Subscription Terms by publishing the change and sending it to
the subscriber's billing contact at least 30 days before a renewal date; the
change applies from that renewal date and not within a running term. A
subscriber may cancel at any time by the self-serve method described in the
Fortress Subscription Terms, with effect from the end of the current term
and without fee. Duplicate subscriptions held by one Organization are
refundable pro rata for any unexpired term.

3.5 Obtaining a subscription. Subscriptions are purchased self-serve at
https://tilion.com/pricing on the Fortress Subscription Terms, accepted by
click-through, without contact with Tilion Inc. Payment methods and order
records are described in those Terms. A subscription key is issued at
purchase; like a free key it is verified on the user's machine and is not
checked online. Permission under Section 3.2(d) begins when the subscription
takes effect or an Order is pending, not on installation of the key.
Eligibility under this Section must be satisfied throughout production use,
subject to the Grace Periods.

3.6 These conditions apply to every version of the Licensed Software
distributed under this License, including where an existing user upgrades
from an earlier BSD-licensed version. Production use of an earlier
BSD-licensed version does not qualify you for free production use of
material under this License beyond the Transition Grace Period. Your rights
in earlier BSD-licensed material are unaffected.

3.7 Permission for production use that is not otherwise permitted begins
when a subscription covering that use takes effect, subject to its payment
and other terms, or when a Grace Period applies. Downloading a copy, or
receiving a copy from a third party, does not itself grant that permission.
A separate written agreement controls over this License for the use it
covers, to the extent of any conflict.

4. Definitions and measurement

4.1 "Organization" means the business or other legal entity using the
Licensed Software, together with every entity that controls it, that it
controls, or that is under common control with it. "Control" means direct
or indirect ownership of more than 50% of the voting interests or the power
to direct management. Financial amounts are aggregated across the
Organization, excluding transactions within the Organization. An investment
fund and its other portfolio companies are not part of an Organization by
reason of the fund's holding alone. A fiscal sponsor or foundation that
holds funds for a project without the power to direct its management does
not control it, and such a project is measured by the funds restricted to
it. An individual conducting a business is an Organization consisting of
that individual alone; production use by an individual that serves, or
whose outputs are provided to, an entity the individual controls is that
entity's use under Section 4.9.

4.2 "Cumulative Funding" means the total gross funding actually received by
the Organization from outside the Organization since its formation,
including equity investments, SAFEs, convertible instruments, debt
financing, and business grants. It excludes revenue from sales of goods or
services, ordinary trade credit, and funding committed but not received.
Spending, repaying, or distributing funds does not reduce Cumulative
Funding. Conversion of an instrument does not count the same funding twice.
A revolving, receivables, or card facility counts at its highest principal
outstanding at any time, not at cumulative draws.

4.3 "Annual Recurring Revenue" or "ARR" means twelve times the recurring
revenue earned in the most recently completed calendar month from
subscriptions and other ongoing customer arrangements, including recurring
usage-based fees. Prepaid recurring fees are allocated evenly over the
period they cover. One-time fees, taxes collected for others, and refunded
amounts are excluded. Fees paid by the same customer for the same or a
substantially similar product or service in the measurement month and in at
least one of the two preceding calendar months are recurring, whatever the
form of the arrangement or the name of the fee. Before the end of the
Organization's first calendar month of operations, ARR is its contracted
monthly recurring fees multiplied by twelve, with longer-period fees
allocated to months. ARR is measured Organization-wide, not only for
revenue attributable to Fortress.

4.4 All thresholds are in United States dollars. Non-USD funding is
converted at the spot exchange rate on the date received; non-USD recurring
revenue is converted at the spot exchange rate on the final day of the
measurement month (or on the measurement date for first-month contracted
fees). The rate source is the US Federal Reserve H.10 rate or the
Organization's central bank reference rate, chosen once and applied
consistently. Thresholds are measured on the first day of each calendar
month and hold for that month.

4.5 "Production use" means executing or operating the Licensed Software in
live business operations, including internal operational automation and
providing operational products, services, or data to customers or other
third parties. Copying, modifying, or redistributing the Licensed Software
without operating it in production is not production use. An Organization
whose product, service, or agent launches and drives the Licensed Software
on a customer's or user's machine as a function of that product is
operating it in production, in addition to any production use by that
customer. The name or location of a deployment does not determine whether
its use is production.

4.6 "Non-production use" means development, testing, evaluation, proofs of
concept, demonstrations, education, and research that do not serve live
business operations or deliver operational products, services, or data.
Non-production use is free for every Organization regardless of funding or
ARR, without registration, contact, or payment. These examples do not limit
the permission for non-production use in Section 2.

4.7 "Fortress Subscription Terms" means the terms accepted at purchase or
renewal of a Fortress subscription, as published at https://tilion.com/terms
on the day of that purchase or renewal.

4.8 "Order" means a purchase created through the checkout page, the API, or
the command-line tool that names the legal entity and a billing contact. An
Order is pending from creation until it is paid, cancelled, or 60 days old,
whichever comes first.

4.9 On-behalf-of rule. Where you make production use of the Licensed
Software on behalf of another Organization, including as an employee,
contractor, consultant, agency, outsourcer, or service provider, that
Organization must also satisfy Section 3 or be covered by a subscription for
that production use. A customer's receipt of your generally available
product or service alone does not make your use "on behalf of" that
customer; operating a dedicated deployment or performing contracted
automation for that customer does.

  (a) A product or service is generally available only where it is offered
      to the public on published terms. Use of a generally available product
      or service is the seller's use alone, even where each customer
      supplies its own inputs, such as targets, search terms, or
      credentials, and receives its own results; those customers are not
      clients under this Section.

  (b) A deployment is dedicated to a customer only where that customer,
      rather than your generally available product, specifies its
      automation targets, workflows, or code. Per-customer isolation,
      configuration, or hosting of a generally available product is not a
      dedicated deployment.

  (c) A customer that, alone or with its Organization, supplied more than
      half of your revenue from a product or service in the measurement
      month, or that formed or funds you, is a client under this Section
      and its use is contracted automation.

  (d) "Client" means the Organization whose live business operations a
      dedicated deployment or contracted automation ultimately serves,
      through any chain of contracts. "Provider" means every Organization
      between the client and the Organization executing the Licensed
      Software. Such use must be permitted as both the provider's use and
      the client's use: each of them that meets the Paid-License Trigger
      requires its own subscription, and a subscription in the client's
      name may be purchased by either party.

  (e) A client that does not itself execute the Licensed Software is not in
      breach for a provider's use of which it had no knowledge. Its
      obligation for that use begins on the earlier of its actual knowledge
      and Tilion Inc.'s written notice to it, and it may cure by obtaining a
      subscription within 30 days of that date.

4.10 Grace Periods. Production use during a Grace Period is permitted under
Section 3.2(c). The Grace Periods are:

  (a) Evaluation: an Organization may make production use for 15 days from
      its first production use of any version of the Licensed Software
      distributed under this License, measured once for the whole
      Organization and not restarted by a later version, release, or
      rebuild. For use under Section 4.9 the period is the provider's, runs
      once from the provider's first production use for any client, and
      covers a client only while the client's own period is also running.

  (b) Crossing: each time an Organization first meets the Paid-License
      Trigger, not having met it on the first day of the preceding month,
      it may continue production use for 15 days from the end of the month
      in which the Paid-License Trigger was met, provided that at least
      twelve months have passed since the end of its last Crossing period.
      A crossing before the Organization's first production use does not
      count.

  (c) Lapse: a subscription that ends for non-payment or a missed renewal
      remains current for 30 days after the end of its term. Production use
      in that period is licensed and, if the subscription is not restored,
      is billed at the monthly list price without uplift.

  (d) Transition: an Organization making production use of an earlier
      BSD-licensed version on the Effective Date has 15 days from the
      Effective Date.

4.11 A Grace Period extends while an Order covering the use is pending, if
the Order was created before the period would otherwise end. An Order
extends a Grace Period once per Organization; later Orders for the same
Organization do not extend it, and no extension runs past 60 days after the
first Order's creation. Each Grace Period arises once per Organization,
except Crossing as provided in Section 4.10(b). Where more than one Grace
Period applies to the same use they run concurrently and the use is
permitted until the latest of their end dates; no Grace Period is added to
the end of another.

